00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:26.400
GANG CHEN: OK, I wish you
all had a good weekend.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:32.460
And just a recap
of last lecture--

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.700
it's a little bit extra
than last lecture--

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:40.350
but to say what we
solved is a problem

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:45.960
for heat conduction along a
two-dimensional, or thin, film.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:51.480
And the Boltzmann equation,
reading for the deviation

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:57.540
function g, g is f minus f0.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.710
So g is f minus f0.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:07.150
And we say g because the
boundary in the y direction,

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:12.040
so there are constraints
changing g in the y direction.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:16.410
So we have the cosine theta--

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.720
cosine theta is
the axial direction

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.870
of the phonon propagation.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:29.670
And so we have, on the left-hand
side is a function of y.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:34.170
And then, on the right-hand
side, it's a function of x.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:40.350
The f0, because I
have f0 for phonon

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:45.030
is exponential
[INAUDIBLE] kt minus 1.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.130
So t is a function of x.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:50.640
Because I would consider
heat conduction along

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.540
the x direction.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.530
And if you do electron
transport, which

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:58.440
we didn't do last
time, you have to keep

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.570
this term, the fourth term.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:03.240
And that's one we
did in chapter 6,

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.950
the thermoelectric transport.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:06.840
We traded both terms.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.229
But see, the point
is if you look

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:15.120
at transport along
the x direction

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.430
and on the right-hand side
is a function of x only.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:24.690
So I have a first-order
differential equation

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.990
for the deviation function
in the y direction

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:32.790
with right-hand
side is x dependent.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:35.610
So because of that, the right
hand is essentially constant.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.610
Because the left-hand
side is a function of y.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:42.190
So when I did the integration,
I find the heat flux.

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:47.310
And what I'm given here is more
than I show in the last lecture

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:51.830
because this solution
is a Fuchs-Sondheimer--

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:01.690
Fuchs-Sond, I checked
the name, heimer--

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:02.190
solution.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.830
and Fuchs-Sondheimer
originally solved

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:09.780
for the electron transport.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.470
So this were, in the original
paper, electric conductivity

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:19.860
ratio of the thin film divided
the bulk is, say, 1 minus.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:21.420
So it's reduced.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:25.930
And this reduction is this
integral mu is cosine theta.

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:27.810
So it's 0 to 1.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:31.950
And the p is a
specularity parameter--

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.800
p specularity parameter.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.740
So if it's p equals
1, it's a specular.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.900
P equals 0 is a diffuse.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:44.430
So you can say if the
specular 1 minus p is 0,

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:47.760
so it's more like a waveguide.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:52.560
There's no reduction if
it's a specular interface.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.180
But the p equals 0 is diffuse,
so you will get reduction

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.140
in the thermal conductivity,
or electric conductivity,

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:05.820
if the interface scattered
electron-phonon diffusely.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:11.640
But say it has been,
actually, really difficult

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:15.210
to say, what is this value of p?

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:17.760
And so far, the
best model people

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:24.000
use was still result that
derived by Ziman in 1950s.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.590
And this p is related
to the surface roughness

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:33.270
and the wavelengths of
the phonon or electron.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.320
So it's an exponential function.

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:38.640
And in fact, you see in
the front it's 16 pi cubic.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:42.660
So that's a pretty large number.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:47.700
Typically, in optics, when
people say a surface is rough,

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:52.500
or it's a specular, for
optically smooth surface,

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:58.010
its typical roughness is less
than 1/10 of the wavelength.

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:01.850
So you can say if the
roughness is less than 1/10

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:12.180
of the wavelength, this
p is getting close to--

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:17.570
this one, when it's very
small, is close to 1.

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:19.820
That's specular.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:23.720
So this is a
Fuchs-Sondheimer solution.

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:29.180
And that's the for the
electrical and thermal

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:33.410
conductivity along a film.

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:36.380
And then we assume that
the length of this film

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:37.470
is very long.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:40.170
So if you go to look
at the literature,

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:42.230
there are a lot of
papers published

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:45.170
on measuring thermal
conductivity of thin films

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:47.420
or nanowires.

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:51.693
And there are also
refined model of wires

00:05:51.693 --> 00:05:53.735
of different cross-section,
whether it's circular

00:05:53.735 --> 00:05:55.640
or whether it is a rectangle.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:58.880
And the people have tried to
solve the Boltzmann equations.

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:03.020
And I mentioned that
in the last lecture,

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:06.860
even now, there are still some
results on the wires, possibly,

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:08.450
and people still
writing what could

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:18.320
be deviation from the Fuchs
and the Sondheimer solution.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:23.750
And historically,
this is Casimir.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:29.000
What Casimir did is,
really, he explained that

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.970
without solving
Boltzmann equation--

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:36.020
just no internal scattering,
you have rough surface--

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:38.870
and he say, OK, if you
have a rough surface

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:43.820
because the scattering
is diffused, so you have,

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:47.870
effectively, reduction in the
mean free path of the heat

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:49.280
carrier of the phonons.

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:53.180
And with that, he
was able to explain

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:56.780
why thermal conductivity
at low temperature

00:06:56.780 --> 00:06:58.700
is proportional
to specific heat.

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:00.920
The mean free paths are
limited by the sample

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.683
size and the low temperature.

00:07:03.683 --> 00:07:05.600
[INAUDIBLE] the thermal
conductivity ones that

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:07.010
are receiving gamma.

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:09.605
So when the mean free
path is a sample size,

00:07:09.605 --> 00:07:12.870
it is proportional
to the specific heat,

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:14.840
which is t cubic.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:19.670
So this is the Casimir
[INAUDIBLE] started.

00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:25.240
So that's last lecture is film
conduction along the film.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:26.470
Just one more comment.

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:29.440
You actually could say
that recently, there

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:33.700
are some paper on the thermal
conductivity of carbon

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:35.110
nanotubes.

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:39.550
And it turns out that
because the thermal nanotube

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:41.350
or the mean free
path is even longer

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:44.530
than the length of
the tube, so there

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:48.670
is additional sizing factor
at the two ends of the tube.

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:55.570
And what are we going
to do in today's lecture

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:59.680
is look at the other
direction, so heat conduction

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:02.650
perpendicular.

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:09.490
Let's say transport
perpendicular to film.

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:32.640
And if I look at
now the problem is,

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:35.970
and if I have a
temperature difference,

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:40.679
I have to be a
little bit ambivalent

00:08:40.679 --> 00:08:43.419
at this stage, what's this
temperature really mean.

00:08:43.419 --> 00:08:51.060
So I have a Te1, Te2 in the
thickness direction of a film.

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:52.350
And this is my y.

00:08:56.784 --> 00:09:01.080
And again, I have
the mean free path is

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:04.380
could be longer than
the film thickness.

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:10.940
And then what's my effective
thermal conductivity?

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:12.770
And you can ask the
same question-- what's

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:14.900
my electrical conductivity?

00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:20.930
And also, there's solution for
radiation, photon transport,

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:25.400
all those under
the same equation.

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:30.140
And I write down the Boltzmann
equation on the relaxation time

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:31.410
approximation.

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:44.030
So here, I have vy df dy
equals minus f minus f0 divided

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:44.630
by tau.

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:52.830
Because in this case, my
equivalent temperature

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:54.960
changes as a function of y.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:10:00.030
So T is effectively
a function of y.

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:05.980
And f0 now, through the
temperature dependence,

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:10.852
exponential h bar
omega kT minus 1.

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:19.420
So you can say,
well, the difference

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:24.380
from the previous problem
is in the previous problem,

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:27.220
the relaxation time
is a function of x,

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:31.620
but the size effect
happens in the y direction.

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:34.670
Now my temperature
is a function of y,

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:38.690
and the size happen
in the same direction.

00:10:38.690 --> 00:10:42.280
So my solution will
be more complicated

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:44.240
than the previous one.

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:46.600
And let's solve this problem.

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:52.780
And I will first do vy, again,
say any phonon transport

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:55.880
direction-- let's say
along this direction--

00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:58.780
and the angle to the
y direction is theta.

00:10:58.780 --> 00:11:02.365
So I will do this vy
is v cosine theta.

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:10.670
And v times tau I will normalize
against the mean free path.

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:14.230
So mean free path
is v times tau.

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:17.200
And I define a
non-dimensional variable eta

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:19.390
equals y over mean free path.

00:11:22.370 --> 00:11:25.460
And in the previous
one, I could say

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:27.600
equals d over mean free path.

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:30.020
So that's normalized
to the thickness.

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:32.990
But this is an arbitrary
coordinate of y.

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:37.460
And if I do this, I write
the Boltzmann equation now

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:42.200
into mu, which is cosine theta.

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:46.040
Mu equals cosine theta that's
the direction of cosine.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:55.550
And I have df dy plus f, I move
to the right-- not y, d eta--

00:11:55.550 --> 00:12:00.080
the mean free path
now is v times tau.

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:04.430
So say eta is y
over mean free path.

00:12:04.430 --> 00:12:07.220
So I non-dimensionalize
my equation.

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:11.000
On the right-hand
side, then, I have f0.

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:17.380
Remember, f0 is a function of
T, and T is a function of y.

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:27.400
I do not know that T. So now,
if I say the function of y

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:32.590
is also a function of
eta, so I have the f

00:12:32.590 --> 00:12:34.210
is a function of eta.

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:38.930
F0 is also a function of eta.

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:41.590
Now, this equation is
a little bit harder

00:12:41.590 --> 00:12:44.770
to solve than the previous one.

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:47.770
Because in the previous
one, the right-hand side

00:12:47.770 --> 00:12:48.970
is a function of x.

00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:51.340
The left-hand side
is a function of y.

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:55.870
Now, in the current one, both
left- and right-hand sides

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:57.220
are a function of eta.

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:02.930
And to solve this, the
first-order differential

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:05.870
equation, the trick
you do is, first,

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:08.000
you solve homogeneous equation--

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:13.790
eta df d eta plus f equals 0.

00:13:13.790 --> 00:13:16.550
And here, you'll
find out this one

00:13:16.550 --> 00:13:23.480
gives you f either is a
function of C exponential

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:28.210
minus eta over mu.

00:13:28.210 --> 00:13:31.000
That's the solution for
homogeneous equation

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:32.545
when the right-hand side is 0.

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:37.640
And now, when the
right-hand side

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:42.230
is a function of eta
itself, you change

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:45.880
that C into an unknown function
you want is a function of eta.

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:48.110
So that's the standard
way of solving.

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:50.450
Now, rather than
say it's a constant,

00:13:50.450 --> 00:13:53.660
say it's a function of eta.

00:13:53.660 --> 00:13:58.290
You substitute this back
into the original equation.

00:13:58.290 --> 00:14:02.680
You'll find out that
this term will drop out.

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:12.840
So what I have is mu
exponential eta mu dC d eta

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:18.130
equals f0, which is
a function of eta.

00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:22.380
So this, when you substitute
this back into the differential

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:24.960
equation, this term drops.

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:28.770
The other term-- you can take a
derivative of this-- also drop.

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:34.320
The C term I cannot,
so it's there.

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:37.200
And now, because this
is a function of eta,

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:40.000
this is a function of
eta, so I can integrate.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:44.700
So what I have is C itself.

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:53.880
dC, d eta equals f0, which
is a function of T eta--

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:55.800
that's a function of T--

00:14:55.800 --> 00:15:02.220
and y over mu
exponential eta mu.

00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:03.900
So I integrate.

00:15:03.900 --> 00:15:12.060
I will get a C is C integrate--
another integration constant.

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:14.520
That depends on where I start.

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:28.740
So this is my eta 0 to eta
and 1 over mu exponential eta

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:36.190
prime and f0 eta
prime d eta prime.

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:42.480
So this is my solution
for this constant, which

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:43.810
is now no longer constant.

00:15:43.810 --> 00:15:45.600
It's a function of eta.

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:47.040
And that's my solution.

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:52.530
And I combine this C
back into the original,

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:58.950
my solution for f eta.

00:15:58.950 --> 00:16:02.400
But it's also a
direction function of mu

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:07.800
is C0 exponential
minus eta over mu.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:09.780
That's this term.

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:14.080
Now, this term is also
multiplied into this term.

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:17.940
So what I have, because if this
y is integrated at eta prime,

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:20.020
so I can put that in.

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:28.560
So what I have is eta 0 to eta
1 over mu exponential minus eta

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:35.520
minus eta prime
mu f0 d eta prime.

00:16:38.530 --> 00:16:45.070
So that's my general solution
for this original differential

00:16:45.070 --> 00:16:45.580
equation.

00:16:45.580 --> 00:16:48.220
You see that I didn't go
to find the deviation.

00:16:48.220 --> 00:16:51.970
I just solved f directly.

00:16:51.970 --> 00:16:56.290
And for the previous
problem, this one,

00:16:56.290 --> 00:16:57.880
I used a division function.

00:16:57.880 --> 00:16:58.810
But you don't have to.

00:16:58.810 --> 00:17:00.550
You can still solve f directly.

00:17:00.550 --> 00:17:04.035
You get the same result. It's
just a math book difference.

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:11.630
And again, I need to now use
the boundary condition to solve

00:17:11.630 --> 00:17:16.910
for C0 and for each mu.

00:17:16.910 --> 00:17:20.060
Now, for the
problem, each mu, mu

00:17:20.060 --> 00:17:25.470
is cosine theta so that
for each theta, I have,

00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:28.200
say, a different origin.

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:31.460
So for theta between
0 to 90 degree.

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:32.840
I have y equals 0.

00:17:32.840 --> 00:17:36.740
This surface, if it's
diffuse, the temperature Te1

00:17:36.740 --> 00:17:41.410
that goes out is isotropic.

00:17:41.410 --> 00:17:44.170
And then, for 90
degree to 100 degree,

00:17:44.170 --> 00:17:47.780
it's actually coming
from this surface.

00:17:47.780 --> 00:17:49.940
And again, say, it's diffuse.

00:17:49.940 --> 00:17:53.450
The temperature that
goes out is Te2.

00:17:53.450 --> 00:17:57.740
This is what I call
emitted temperature.

00:17:57.740 --> 00:18:04.910
So I go to do the condition
where eta equals 0.

00:18:04.910 --> 00:18:09.530
So I start with eta 0 also at 0.

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:11.280
And this one drops out.

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:17.780
So what I have is f for mu is
less than 1 larger than 0--

00:18:17.780 --> 00:18:19.700
that's 0 to 90 degree--

00:18:19.700 --> 00:18:34.070
f eta mu is f0 Te1
exponential minus eta

00:18:34.070 --> 00:18:43.010
to mu plus 0 to eta 1 over mu
exponential minus eta minus eta

00:18:43.010 --> 00:18:46.970
prime over mu f0 d eta prime.

00:18:50.080 --> 00:18:52.990
So unfortunately,
the difficulty is

00:18:52.990 --> 00:18:58.030
this is almost the simplest
heat conduction problem.

00:18:58.030 --> 00:19:00.490
It becomes very involved.

00:19:00.490 --> 00:19:06.730
So this is the solution
for 0 to mu to 1.

00:19:06.730 --> 00:19:09.380
I will put it a positive.

00:19:09.380 --> 00:19:14.450
That's from bottom up,
forward propagating.

00:19:14.450 --> 00:19:21.860
And similarly, for 90 degree to
180 degree, I have a solution.

00:19:25.850 --> 00:19:33.710
For 90 to 180, so
mu is negative 1

00:19:33.710 --> 00:19:41.810
and to 0, that's theta less
than, say, 180 degree, larger

00:19:41.810 --> 00:19:47.320
than 90 degree, so backward
propagating from top surface

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:48.520
to down.

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:55.180
And my solution, f
prime eta mu, then you

00:19:55.180 --> 00:20:02.350
would say f Te2, that's the
corresponding Bose-Einstein

00:20:02.350 --> 00:20:05.800
distribution for
the phonons that

00:20:05.800 --> 00:20:10.840
are coming into the domain
with a current temperature Te2.

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:14.260
So this is f0 Te2.

00:20:14.260 --> 00:20:22.450
And exponential xi
minus eta over mu, this

00:20:22.450 --> 00:20:25.880
is the effective
boundary effect.

00:20:25.880 --> 00:20:28.670
Here is eta our mu.

00:20:28.670 --> 00:20:30.040
It's negative.

00:20:30.040 --> 00:20:31.820
It's exponential decay.

00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:39.480
So what it says is if
my phonon is propagating

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:44.810
in this direction from the
boundary at this point,

00:20:44.810 --> 00:20:47.780
because of scatter, it decays.

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:52.880
And that distance, the
decay that is exponential,

00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:54.660
xi minus eta--

00:20:54.660 --> 00:21:01.040
so that's really d minus
y, so that's this section.

00:21:01.040 --> 00:21:04.030
And divided by cosine,
divided by mu is cosine theta.

00:21:04.030 --> 00:21:05.030
So that's this distance.

00:21:07.610 --> 00:21:11.570
So it's really because
mean free path,

00:21:11.570 --> 00:21:15.260
so it's how much distance
travel over mean free path

00:21:15.260 --> 00:21:16.700
exponential.

00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:19.890
That's the decay because
they scatter out.

00:21:19.890 --> 00:21:24.320
The boundary terms get scattered
and lost to the internal.

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:33.440
And then the last term
is xi to et and the 1

00:21:33.440 --> 00:21:38.870
over mu exponential minus--

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:43.730
here, I have to be careful
because this is xi to eta,

00:21:43.730 --> 00:21:50.900
so any d eta prime term, I have
to make sure this is a decay.

00:21:50.900 --> 00:21:57.080
So I have eta minus eta prime.

00:21:57.080 --> 00:21:59.810
That will be-- wait,
let me see whether it's

00:21:59.810 --> 00:22:01.320
positive or negative.

00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:05.640
So any term in between this
will be negative, this negative.

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:08.330
I should have another
negative sign in the front

00:22:08.330 --> 00:22:13.770
and the f0 eta prime.

00:22:13.770 --> 00:22:15.920
So this is how I
look at the equation

00:22:15.920 --> 00:22:19.250
while I see whether my
solution is reasonable.

00:22:19.250 --> 00:22:24.950
What I have here is I'm
looking at a certain point--

00:22:24.950 --> 00:22:27.920
this point-- in space.

00:22:27.920 --> 00:22:31.350
I look at the distribution
in this specific direction.

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:32.180
And what it comes--

00:22:32.180 --> 00:22:35.300
one part is coming from
the wall, the boundary.

00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:37.680
And that part of the
exponential is scattered out.

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:38.870
So that's the first term.

00:22:38.870 --> 00:22:41.330
That's the decay term.

00:22:41.330 --> 00:22:43.730
This term is decaying.

00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:46.250
The outer term is,
along the path,

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:54.560
every point is a ray scatter
into this direction, like, say,

00:22:54.560 --> 00:22:57.840
emission, an emission
into this direction.

00:22:57.840 --> 00:23:01.130
So because of this,
I have contribution.

00:23:01.130 --> 00:23:04.400
The contribution from this
point, that's f eta the prime--

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:09.020
f0 eta prime--
that's regenerated.

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:13.500
And the distance is
here, the difference.

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:17.920
So this is a regenerated phonon.

00:23:17.920 --> 00:23:21.310
And this is the distance
divided by cosine theta, so

00:23:21.310 --> 00:23:23.560
the real distance.

00:23:23.560 --> 00:23:25.930
The y direction
coordinate, the y by cosine

00:23:25.930 --> 00:23:30.460
say that that's the real
distance between this source

00:23:30.460 --> 00:23:34.330
point and the point
I'm interested in.

00:23:34.330 --> 00:23:37.760
And again, during this travel,
there is a scattering out.

00:23:37.760 --> 00:23:39.490
So that's why they
exponent I have

00:23:39.490 --> 00:23:43.900
to make is make sure
this exponent at the end

00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:46.300
is negative eta minus eta prime.

00:23:46.300 --> 00:23:49.660
Because this is eta xi.

00:23:49.660 --> 00:23:52.450
So the eta prime is
between eta and xi.

00:23:52.450 --> 00:23:53.920
So this is negative.

00:23:53.920 --> 00:23:55.370
And mu is negative.

00:23:55.370 --> 00:23:57.310
So I put the negative sign.

00:23:57.310 --> 00:23:59.320
That should be my
correct solution.

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:06.800
You can go to actually
check the mathematics.

00:24:06.800 --> 00:24:12.050
So now I have the
distribution function.

00:24:12.050 --> 00:24:17.360
And my challenge is,
remember, this one, f0,

00:24:17.360 --> 00:24:19.760
is a function of [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:19.760 --> 00:24:22.010
I do not know.

00:24:22.010 --> 00:24:24.760
It's inside the integral.

00:24:24.760 --> 00:24:28.680
And what I have is a
non-local transport.

00:24:28.680 --> 00:24:32.040
It's not like the Fourier's
law of heat conduction

00:24:32.040 --> 00:24:37.530
where q equals minus k dt dx.

00:24:37.530 --> 00:24:40.230
You see this is a
local transport.

00:24:40.230 --> 00:24:44.930
Because the heat flux is
proportional to local gradient.

00:24:44.930 --> 00:24:47.240
It doesn't depend on
quantity other place.

00:24:47.240 --> 00:24:49.790
Of course, they are all coupled.

00:24:49.790 --> 00:24:53.990
But local flux is only
proportional local gradient.

00:24:53.990 --> 00:24:59.210
What I have here is
the flux at this point

00:24:59.210 --> 00:25:01.520
depends on every point
along the trajectory.

00:25:01.520 --> 00:25:06.210
That's why I have the
integral-- path integral-- here.

00:25:06.210 --> 00:25:09.040
So this is the problem
when you go through nano.

00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:14.880
Your transport becomes
coupled, and it's non-local.

00:25:14.880 --> 00:25:19.740
And now, once I have f, I can
solve for my flux same way

00:25:19.740 --> 00:25:29.700
q equals 1 over v. You sum up
all polarization, kx, ky, kz,

00:25:29.700 --> 00:25:34.800
and vy, h bar omega f.

00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:37.710
That's always the
starting point when

00:25:37.710 --> 00:25:41.910
we try to find out what's
the [INAUDIBLE] flux.

00:25:41.910 --> 00:25:44.070
Here, it's the full heat flux.

00:25:44.070 --> 00:25:48.480
Again, charge flux, momentum
flux, this is how we start.

00:25:48.480 --> 00:25:52.140
We sum up all possible modes--

00:25:52.140 --> 00:25:54.270
quantum mechanical
or classical modes.

00:25:54.270 --> 00:25:57.040
And together, they--
and, of course,

00:25:57.040 --> 00:25:59.670
given the quantity h
bar omega, h [INAUDIBLE]

00:25:59.670 --> 00:26:04.840
carry, quantum state carry,
and the group velocity.

00:26:04.840 --> 00:26:10.140
So if you do this and you see we
have to convert this summation

00:26:10.140 --> 00:26:13.020
into integration.

00:26:13.020 --> 00:26:20.370
And I will actually split
this into two terms--

00:26:20.370 --> 00:26:23.280
positive term and negative term.

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:26.610
And positive term are the
ones that goes forward.

00:26:26.610 --> 00:26:29.220
Negative terms are going
from the outer boundary

00:26:29.220 --> 00:26:31.350
going downward.

00:26:31.350 --> 00:26:34.440
And then we can look at
what the expression after I

00:26:34.440 --> 00:26:41.190
convert this into from
summation into integration.

00:26:41.190 --> 00:26:48.390
So positive term is
actually 0 to pi d phi.

00:26:48.390 --> 00:26:51.600
So this is-- again,
I'm changing this

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:54.590
into a spherical coordinate.

00:26:54.590 --> 00:26:57.640
And phi is azimuthal angle.

00:26:57.640 --> 00:27:04.490
And 0 to 90 degree
sine theta d theta--

00:27:07.300 --> 00:27:10.080
theta is the angle,
the polar angle,

00:27:10.080 --> 00:27:13.980
under my coordinate system.

00:27:13.980 --> 00:27:20.580
And then I have integration over
frequency, 0 to max frequency,

00:27:20.580 --> 00:27:22.390
and h bar omega--

00:27:22.390 --> 00:27:26.970
so that's here--
v cosine theta--

00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:28.770
that's vy.

00:27:28.770 --> 00:27:32.400
And when I convert some
[INAUDIBLE] integration,

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:37.050
I have density of
states, D omega over 4 pi

00:27:37.050 --> 00:27:39.540
because this D omega is
integrated originally

00:27:39.540 --> 00:27:42.990
for 4 pi, isotropic case.

00:27:42.990 --> 00:27:47.280
And then I have f plus--

00:27:47.280 --> 00:27:52.020
that's the solution
I have there, here.

00:27:52.020 --> 00:27:56.490
f plus is a function
of both mu and eta.

00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:59.910
And then integrate
over frequency.

00:28:03.390 --> 00:28:10.900
So that's the flux here.

00:28:10.900 --> 00:28:17.910
And if you plug in your
solution for f eta plus,

00:28:17.910 --> 00:28:22.800
what we have here, and
we can write it into--

00:28:22.800 --> 00:28:26.100
so I'll give you the
final expression.

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:39.772
And this one, what I have
is 2 pi I0 Te1 times E3.

00:28:42.500 --> 00:28:47.360
And this E3 is, again, the
integral exponential function

00:28:47.360 --> 00:28:52.220
that we introduced in the
last lecture and 2 pi 0

00:28:52.220 --> 00:29:06.440
to eta I0 eta prime and E2 eta
minus eta prime d eta prime.

00:29:06.440 --> 00:29:11.510
So I have to, now,
introduce this I0.

00:29:11.510 --> 00:29:15.180
I is a transformation.

00:29:15.180 --> 00:29:23.130
So I0 is just the
integration of h bar omega

00:29:23.130 --> 00:29:32.030
and v times D over
4 pi and f0 d omega.

00:29:32.030 --> 00:29:35.360
So sometimes, say, this
is called intensity.

00:29:43.190 --> 00:29:48.350
But it's just a result coming
from the simplification

00:29:48.350 --> 00:29:49.610
in notation.

00:29:49.610 --> 00:29:52.430
And in fact, in
photon transport,

00:29:52.430 --> 00:29:55.670
this intensity is widely used.

00:29:55.670 --> 00:29:58.920
But for electron and
phonon transport,

00:29:58.920 --> 00:30:00.330
this is not widely used.

00:30:00.330 --> 00:30:02.960
I remember when I
first wrote a paper

00:30:02.960 --> 00:30:05.660
and submitted it Phys Review B.
And the Review had come back,

00:30:05.660 --> 00:30:07.800
what is intensity?

00:30:07.800 --> 00:30:12.170
And is it a scalar
or is it a vector?

00:30:12.170 --> 00:30:15.830
And I mentioned before
this relate to f itself.

00:30:15.830 --> 00:30:18.650
Is f itself a scalar or vector?

00:30:18.650 --> 00:30:20.840
It has directionality.

00:30:20.840 --> 00:30:23.100
It depends on space.

00:30:23.100 --> 00:30:28.670
But it's scalar because it's
a scalar in the phase space.

00:30:28.670 --> 00:30:32.210
And the directionality came
from the velocity component

00:30:32.210 --> 00:30:34.290
in the phase space.

00:30:34.290 --> 00:30:40.220
So this is, again,
it's a scalar.

00:30:40.220 --> 00:30:43.340
So this is the
positive heat flux.

00:30:43.340 --> 00:30:48.875
And you can integrate the f
minus and together [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:48.875 --> 00:30:49.910
heat flux.

00:30:49.910 --> 00:30:53.790
And the [INAUDIBLE] flux is
the difference of the two.

00:30:53.790 --> 00:30:56.060
So if I write the lengthy flux--

00:30:56.060 --> 00:30:57.320
let me write it down--

00:30:57.320 --> 00:31:01.250
and you will gather
the [INAUDIBLE] flux

00:31:01.250 --> 00:31:06.844
q is q plus minus q minus.

00:31:06.844 --> 00:31:21.510
And what I have here is
2 pi and I0 Te1 E3 eta--

00:31:21.510 --> 00:31:22.485
that's the first term--

00:31:25.020 --> 00:31:31.200
plus what we just
wrote down, 0 to eta I0

00:31:31.200 --> 00:31:38.370
eta prime and E2 eta minus
eta prime g eta prime.

00:31:38.370 --> 00:31:40.750
So that's the plus term.

00:31:40.750 --> 00:31:42.600
And then I have minus term--

00:31:42.600 --> 00:32:02.950
minus I0 Te2 E3 and xi minus
eta minus eta to xi and I0

00:32:02.950 --> 00:32:12.920
eta prime and E2 eta
prime minus eta d prime.

00:32:12.920 --> 00:32:18.790
So this were the end
result after you substitute

00:32:18.790 --> 00:32:22.810
q plus, which we derived,
and q minus, which

00:32:22.810 --> 00:32:23.890
we didn't write down.

00:32:27.260 --> 00:32:29.900
So this was your
[INAUDIBLE] flux.

00:32:29.900 --> 00:32:32.270
This term come from boundary.

00:32:32.270 --> 00:32:34.490
This term comes from boundary.

00:32:34.490 --> 00:32:38.480
This term's coming
from internal points

00:32:38.480 --> 00:32:42.530
the non-local part, the source,
and how much they contribute

00:32:42.530 --> 00:32:46.250
to the [INAUDIBLE] flux,
either the location, eta,

00:32:46.250 --> 00:32:47.840
that I'm considering.

00:32:47.840 --> 00:32:54.350
So this one, q is a function
of eta, the different locations

00:32:54.350 --> 00:32:58.260
along the y direction.

00:32:58.260 --> 00:32:58.800
Yes?

00:32:58.800 --> 00:33:01.500
AUDIENCE: How can you know
the effective temperature

00:33:01.500 --> 00:33:02.793
distribution [INAUDIBLE]?

00:33:02.793 --> 00:33:03.960
GANG CHEN: I don't know yet.

00:33:03.960 --> 00:33:06.070
I didn't tell you I know.

00:33:06.070 --> 00:33:09.350
That's what we want to find.

00:33:09.350 --> 00:33:12.110
So this is the
complexity of solving

00:33:12.110 --> 00:33:18.080
even the simplest [INAUDIBLE]
problem, non-local problems.

00:33:18.080 --> 00:33:19.790
So what I'm going to next do--

00:33:19.790 --> 00:33:21.470
this is you asked
the question-- so

00:33:21.470 --> 00:33:23.240
how I'm going to find this out?

00:33:26.140 --> 00:33:30.950
So my next step is to
say, to find this out,

00:33:30.950 --> 00:33:32.920
I have to say the heat flux--

00:33:32.920 --> 00:33:36.200
heat flux, if there is no
internal heat generation,

00:33:36.200 --> 00:33:39.580
whether there is internal
heat generation or not.

00:33:39.580 --> 00:33:43.120
Remember, I'm doing the
steady state problem.

00:33:43.120 --> 00:33:45.015
You would say that,
based on the treatment,

00:33:45.015 --> 00:33:50.550
that when I do transient,
all my analysis doesn't work.

00:33:50.550 --> 00:33:53.970
So well, here, the
analysis is fine.

00:33:53.970 --> 00:33:56.590
But well, my next
step is doing the--

00:34:01.660 --> 00:34:05.710
I say there is a case when I'm
dealing with no internal heat

00:34:05.710 --> 00:34:08.800
generation.

00:34:08.800 --> 00:34:12.850
Now, in fact, we wrote a
paper to compare the case.

00:34:12.850 --> 00:34:14.780
When there is the
internal heat generation,

00:34:14.780 --> 00:34:16.429
what's the difference?

00:34:16.429 --> 00:34:21.190
So with no internal
heat generation--

00:34:21.190 --> 00:34:32.350
no heat generation-- and
in this case, what I have,

00:34:32.350 --> 00:34:33.205
q is constant.

00:34:38.020 --> 00:34:43.030
If q is constant,
I can do dq d eta

00:34:43.030 --> 00:34:52.989
equals 0 because I don't need
to find that constant yet.

00:34:52.989 --> 00:34:58.290
So with that, I take
this dq d eta equals 0.

00:34:58.290 --> 00:35:02.610
I will find the equation
that actually has--

00:35:02.610 --> 00:35:08.210
either they're eta 0 or,
say, I0 that I want to find.

00:35:08.210 --> 00:35:10.200
So you go to take
the derivative.

00:35:10.200 --> 00:35:13.580
Let me just repeat
here, what is the E.

00:35:13.580 --> 00:35:22.550
En, the n-th order integral
equation equals, say,

00:35:22.550 --> 00:35:30.170
t n minus 2 exponential
minus t over mu and d--

00:35:30.170 --> 00:35:30.890
no, not t.

00:35:30.890 --> 00:35:33.950
This is mu and d mu 0 to 1.

00:35:33.950 --> 00:35:39.410
This is my integral
function of order n.

00:35:39.410 --> 00:35:42.020
So I have E3.

00:35:42.020 --> 00:35:46.620
And here is E2 you
can substitute in.

00:35:46.620 --> 00:35:56.780
So if I take dq d eta equals 0,
what I have is a equation I0 T1

00:35:56.780 --> 00:36:17.342
and E2 eta plus eta 0 Te2 and E2
xi minus eta and plus 0 to eta,

00:36:17.342 --> 00:36:30.650
eta 0 I0 eta prime E1 eta
minus eta prime absolute d eta

00:36:30.650 --> 00:36:33.590
prime equals 0.

00:36:33.590 --> 00:36:35.570
I didn't go step by step.

00:36:35.570 --> 00:36:40.900
But say when you take a
derivative, E3 becomes E2.

00:36:40.900 --> 00:36:44.760
And the E2 becomes E1.

00:36:44.760 --> 00:36:50.970
And these two half integral--
this is 0 to eta, eta to xi--

00:36:50.970 --> 00:36:55.080
is combined right
into the 0 to xi.

00:36:55.080 --> 00:36:58.470
Inside is I0 eta prime.

00:36:58.470 --> 00:37:00.990
But E1 now has an absolute sign.

00:37:00.990 --> 00:37:05.040
Because it changed sign
when eta equals eta prime.

00:37:05.040 --> 00:37:07.950
So I put a absolute to
simplify the notation.

00:37:07.950 --> 00:37:09.300
These are actually two terms.

00:37:12.370 --> 00:37:15.550
So now, this is the
equation you need

00:37:15.550 --> 00:37:20.140
to solve to actually find, given
the boundary condition on one

00:37:20.140 --> 00:37:23.470
side, the Te1, on the
other side, the Te2,

00:37:23.470 --> 00:37:29.890
and the mean free path film
thickness that determined xi.

00:37:29.890 --> 00:37:32.560
And then you need to solve
this equation to find out

00:37:32.560 --> 00:37:36.440
what is I0 at each point.

00:37:36.440 --> 00:37:40.240
I0, remember, is related
to my temperature.

00:37:40.240 --> 00:37:42.550
I0 is related to f0.

00:37:42.550 --> 00:37:46.090
Because this is the phonon
property [INAUDIBLE]

00:37:46.090 --> 00:37:49.455
frequency omega, which
you know from the solid

00:37:49.455 --> 00:37:50.620
that you're dealing with.

00:37:50.620 --> 00:37:58.480
But f0 is a function of T.
T is what I want to find.

00:37:58.480 --> 00:38:01.420
So you really have to
solve this to find I0.

00:38:01.420 --> 00:38:06.010
From I0, you find T.
This is the complication

00:38:06.010 --> 00:38:11.020
of heat conduction,
non-local heat conduction.

00:38:11.020 --> 00:38:14.200
And it's an integral equation.

00:38:14.200 --> 00:38:19.500
And how many of you have dealt
with integral equation before?

00:38:23.100 --> 00:38:23.600
One?

00:38:26.370 --> 00:38:30.270
So what you need to
find is what's inside

00:38:30.270 --> 00:38:34.860
and how to solve
integral equations.

00:38:34.860 --> 00:38:38.850
And I can simplify
this a little bit

00:38:38.850 --> 00:38:40.740
just to make the equation nicer.

00:38:40.740 --> 00:38:45.450
So let me introduce a variable.

00:38:45.450 --> 00:38:49.560
Theta is a function for eta.

00:38:49.560 --> 00:38:51.960
And that equals
non-dimensionalized.

00:38:51.960 --> 00:38:58.950
So I have I0 eta minus I0 Te2.

00:38:58.950 --> 00:39:06.570
And I have here I0
Te1 minus I0 Te2.

00:39:06.570 --> 00:39:11.550
So I introduce this
non-dimensional temperature,

00:39:11.550 --> 00:39:17.020
or you can say it's temperature
or non-dimensional intensity

00:39:17.020 --> 00:39:23.170
and then say it turns out there
is also non-dimensional heat

00:39:23.170 --> 00:39:35.980
flux, which is the q, the heat
flux over pi I0 Te1 minus I0

00:39:35.980 --> 00:39:38.230
Te2.

00:39:38.230 --> 00:39:41.560
So this is the corresponding
non-dimensional heat flux.

00:39:41.560 --> 00:39:45.100
And with this theta, I
can rewrite the equation.

00:39:45.100 --> 00:39:51.680
You basically substitute this
I0 into here using the reverse

00:39:51.680 --> 00:39:52.180
back.

00:39:52.180 --> 00:39:54.760
And you can rewrite
the integral equation

00:39:54.760 --> 00:39:56.440
into a more standard form.

00:40:08.500 --> 00:40:13.020
This is what you
have is 2 theta eta

00:40:13.020 --> 00:40:23.905
equals E2 eta plus
0 to cosine theta

00:40:23.905 --> 00:40:31.790
eta prime and E1
eta minus eta prime.

00:40:31.790 --> 00:40:34.500
And this is d eta prime.

00:40:34.500 --> 00:40:39.870
So this is the integral
equation, a nicer form.

00:40:39.870 --> 00:40:43.000
Theta-- and this is the
inside of the integrand.

00:40:43.000 --> 00:40:48.400
And there is also non-function,
non-zero non-function.

00:40:48.400 --> 00:40:51.180
And this is called
Fredholm integral equation

00:40:51.180 --> 00:40:53.040
of second kind--

00:40:59.120 --> 00:41:08.090
integral equation
of second kind.

00:41:14.450 --> 00:41:16.970
And when I try to solve
this integral equation,

00:41:16.970 --> 00:41:19.430
I want the numerical recipe.

00:41:19.430 --> 00:41:22.190
And people would
teach you how to solve

00:41:22.190 --> 00:41:23.390
this integral equations.

00:41:23.390 --> 00:41:25.460
There's no analytical solution.

00:41:25.460 --> 00:41:28.328
And what you have here,
E1 is often called,

00:41:28.328 --> 00:41:29.120
this is the kernel.

00:41:34.340 --> 00:41:37.860
That will be multiplied
to the unknown function.

00:41:37.860 --> 00:41:41.280
And in the old days, for
this kernel, people--

00:41:41.280 --> 00:41:43.320
when the computer
would become powerful--

00:41:43.320 --> 00:41:46.135
before that, for
this kernel, people

00:41:46.135 --> 00:41:47.760
would try to get an
analytical solution

00:41:47.760 --> 00:41:48.802
by approximating kernels.

00:41:51.860 --> 00:41:54.680
But nowadays, you can solve
this integral equation

00:41:54.680 --> 00:41:57.110
relatively easily.

00:41:57.110 --> 00:41:59.330
And the way to solve
the integral question

00:41:59.330 --> 00:42:02.060
is going back to
saying if you want

00:42:02.060 --> 00:42:07.280
to do a integral of a function,
what method do you use?

00:42:07.280 --> 00:42:12.450
The simplest method of
doing a numerical integral

00:42:12.450 --> 00:42:14.910
is trapezoidal method.

00:42:14.910 --> 00:42:23.930
So whatever function this
theta is, this is my eta.

00:42:23.930 --> 00:42:26.630
This is the unknown
function I want to find.

00:42:29.150 --> 00:42:30.770
Let's say this is my theta eta.

00:42:33.660 --> 00:42:39.030
And I divide this
domain into endpoint.

00:42:39.030 --> 00:42:45.330
Say here, you can do-- the
trapezoidal is equally divided.

00:42:50.980 --> 00:42:56.350
And for each of this trapezoid--
you take a trapezoid--

00:42:56.350 --> 00:43:01.460
you have your area is
the width times height.

00:43:01.460 --> 00:43:05.110
So I can do my--

00:43:05.110 --> 00:43:08.620
approximate this integral
by discretization--

00:43:08.620 --> 00:43:19.450
0 xi theta eta prime and E1
eta minus eta prime d eta

00:43:19.450 --> 00:43:22.660
prime equals--

00:43:22.660 --> 00:43:26.740
and trapezoid typically have
two ends special because it's

00:43:26.740 --> 00:43:28.920
only half [INAUDIBLE].

00:43:28.920 --> 00:43:39.300
So I have a theta 0 and E1 eta.

00:43:39.300 --> 00:43:45.180
And this is a when theta prime
equals 0, so eta minus 0.

00:43:45.180 --> 00:43:49.770
Eta itself is positive, so I
don't need the absolute sign.

00:43:49.770 --> 00:43:51.510
Delta eta over 2.

00:43:51.510 --> 00:43:55.110
So delta eta is the
separation of the points.

00:43:58.260 --> 00:44:03.300
And I have, similarly, the
other end point, theta xi.

00:44:03.300 --> 00:44:06.900
So this is when eta prime
equals this endpoint,

00:44:06.900 --> 00:44:08.670
I have this have half
[INAUDIBLE] only.

00:44:08.670 --> 00:44:15.450
So I have an E1, and
eta prime equals xi.

00:44:15.450 --> 00:44:25.410
So I have xi minus eta and then
theta eta theta [INAUDIBLE]..

00:44:25.410 --> 00:44:34.800
And then the rest is
the delta eta and 1 to n

00:44:34.800 --> 00:44:37.050
minus one point in the center.

00:44:37.050 --> 00:44:42.630
I have theta eta eta I--

00:44:42.630 --> 00:44:50.100
theta I eta I E1
eta minus eta I.

00:44:50.100 --> 00:44:52.830
So I'm discretizing eta prime.

00:44:52.830 --> 00:44:55.575
So eta is the point
I want the find.

00:44:58.110 --> 00:45:04.230
See, that eta, the eta, eta
is any point of this equation

00:45:04.230 --> 00:45:05.220
is satisfied.

00:45:08.470 --> 00:45:13.940
So this is my
discretized term here.

00:45:13.940 --> 00:45:16.050
And then I want to
do-- if I look at this,

00:45:16.050 --> 00:45:18.440
I have how many points
I have I want to find.

00:45:18.440 --> 00:45:27.840
I have theta 0, theta eta, and
theta I 1 to n minus one point,

00:45:27.840 --> 00:45:29.225
so I have n plus 1 points.

00:45:32.140 --> 00:45:42.260
I want to find theta I eta
I equals 0, 1, 3, endpoint.

00:45:42.260 --> 00:45:44.132
So I have total n plus 1 points.

00:45:48.700 --> 00:45:50.350
Now, you look at the equation.

00:45:50.350 --> 00:45:56.290
You substitute this discretized
term into a equation.

00:45:56.290 --> 00:46:00.200
And this should be
valid for any eta point.

00:46:00.200 --> 00:46:03.790
And then you know you need
a n equation because you--

00:46:03.790 --> 00:46:07.420
you need an n plus 1 equation
because you have n plus 1

00:46:07.420 --> 00:46:08.320
[INAUDIBLE].

00:46:10.860 --> 00:46:13.080
So how you get that
n plus 1 equation?

00:46:13.080 --> 00:46:22.050
You let your eta equals
0 delta eta, 2 delta eta,

00:46:22.050 --> 00:46:24.700
to the total endpoint.

00:46:24.700 --> 00:46:29.490
So you say that your
eta equals eta I. That's

00:46:29.490 --> 00:46:32.480
the discretization point.

00:46:32.480 --> 00:46:36.940
And then you'll get the n
plus 1 numerical equation.

00:46:36.940 --> 00:46:40.260
And with that, you
work the matrix

00:46:40.260 --> 00:46:44.330
to find what your theta I is.

00:46:44.330 --> 00:46:46.910
And this matrix
is different plane

00:46:46.910 --> 00:46:50.120
than you solve Fourier
heat conduction equation

00:46:50.120 --> 00:46:53.110
or Navier-Stokes equation.

00:46:53.110 --> 00:46:55.870
In that case, your
matrix is mostly 0,

00:46:55.870 --> 00:46:59.890
except the diagonal
and the two lines

00:46:59.890 --> 00:47:02.290
adjacent to diagonal direction.

00:47:02.290 --> 00:47:04.720
This matrix is
typically a full matrix

00:47:04.720 --> 00:47:06.355
because every term is non-zero.

00:47:08.960 --> 00:47:11.660
So you need to
invert that matrix

00:47:11.660 --> 00:47:17.630
to find the temperature
distribution or, in this case,

00:47:17.630 --> 00:47:20.780
its non-dimensional
intensity distribution.

00:47:24.870 --> 00:47:29.010
But there is one more trick you
put in there, one more problem.

00:47:29.010 --> 00:47:30.540
That's this kernel.

00:47:30.540 --> 00:47:32.970
Most of the time, when you
solve integral equation,

00:47:32.970 --> 00:47:35.360
this kernel has a problem.

00:47:35.360 --> 00:47:37.650
It's a singular.

00:47:37.650 --> 00:47:44.550
And this is a case, if you
think, when you do your here,

00:47:44.550 --> 00:47:58.100
when eta equals eta I, what
is E1 0 when eta equals eta I?

00:48:00.840 --> 00:48:03.440
E1 0, if you look
at my definition

00:48:03.440 --> 00:48:12.180
for the integral function
here, n equals 1, so mu and 0--

00:48:12.180 --> 00:48:15.726
t equals 0-- so it's
a 1 over mu d mu.

00:48:18.360 --> 00:48:24.390
So what I have is 0 to
1 on 1 over mu d mu.

00:48:24.390 --> 00:48:27.590
1 over mu d mu log mu.

00:48:27.590 --> 00:48:30.910
What's the value at log 0?

00:48:30.910 --> 00:48:32.560
Blows up.

00:48:32.560 --> 00:48:36.390
So I have a singular kernel.

00:48:36.390 --> 00:48:38.100
And I have to deal
with that problem

00:48:38.100 --> 00:48:40.126
when I solve integral equations.

00:48:40.126 --> 00:48:45.498
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] doing,
like, one half [INAUDIBLE]..

00:48:45.498 --> 00:48:47.290
GANG CHEN: Yeah, you
don't need to do that.

00:48:47.290 --> 00:48:49.930
There is a trick to do it.

00:48:49.930 --> 00:48:58.480
So the trick to do this one is
actually 0 to xi and theta eta

00:48:58.480 --> 00:49:07.090
prime E1 eta minus
eta prime d eta prime.

00:49:07.090 --> 00:49:10.700
That's what we want
to do numerically.

00:49:10.700 --> 00:49:16.460
And what I'm going to do is
actually, I'm going to do--

00:49:16.460 --> 00:49:18.200
because this is theta--

00:49:18.200 --> 00:49:22.790
I'm going to subtract
this point out.

00:49:22.790 --> 00:49:24.410
So I don't do that integration.

00:49:24.410 --> 00:49:36.930
I do theta prime minus theta
eta E1 eta minus eta prime d eta

00:49:36.930 --> 00:49:37.600
prime.

00:49:37.600 --> 00:49:41.310
So this, I subtract
that theta eta.

00:49:41.310 --> 00:49:44.700
And of course, that means I
will need to add that back.

00:49:44.700 --> 00:49:47.980
So theta eta is not an
integration variable.

00:49:47.980 --> 00:49:59.280
So do theta eta 0 xi E1 eta
minus eta prime d eta prime.

00:50:02.990 --> 00:50:05.480
So this is just a equivalency.

00:50:05.480 --> 00:50:09.260
Because this term,
I subtract, I add.

00:50:09.260 --> 00:50:11.630
And because I'm doing
this and [INAUDIBLE]

00:50:11.630 --> 00:50:15.020
I can plot this integral,
the kernel itself,

00:50:15.020 --> 00:50:17.840
can be an analytically done.

00:50:17.840 --> 00:50:20.710
And if you do this
integration, you

00:50:20.710 --> 00:50:24.160
find that it doesn't blow up.

00:50:24.160 --> 00:50:25.390
So that's the trick.

00:50:25.390 --> 00:50:29.010
And this one, if you
do it, this term--

00:50:29.010 --> 00:50:37.930
in fact, you will say this is
mu 2 minus exponential minus eta

00:50:37.930 --> 00:50:41.350
over mu and then
minus exponential

00:50:41.350 --> 00:50:44.470
xi minus eta over mu.

00:50:44.470 --> 00:50:46.570
So this integral
doesn't blow up,

00:50:46.570 --> 00:50:50.120
so I solve this
singularity problem.

00:50:50.120 --> 00:50:52.360
And that means your
discretization,

00:50:52.360 --> 00:50:54.740
it's a little bit more complex.

00:50:54.740 --> 00:51:00.340
You have to write this
carefully to deal with this one.

00:51:00.340 --> 00:51:04.630
But overall, now you have, by
incorporating those tricks,

00:51:04.630 --> 00:51:10.160
you can solve this
integral equation.

00:51:10.160 --> 00:51:17.960
And you have to do it
yourself to really--

00:51:17.960 --> 00:51:21.460
there's nothing--
you all know MATLAB.

00:51:21.460 --> 00:51:24.150
MATLAB is good at
the inverting matrix.

00:51:24.150 --> 00:51:26.760
When I do it, I have to
write a Fortran code.

00:51:26.760 --> 00:51:29.340
That's what I know.

00:51:29.340 --> 00:51:33.515
But there are also libraries
to call for inverted matrix.

00:51:36.390 --> 00:51:40.720
So this is the
trapezoidal method.

00:51:40.720 --> 00:51:43.020
And if you use
trapezoidal method,

00:51:43.020 --> 00:51:46.050
you need a lot of points.

00:51:46.050 --> 00:51:48.780
For integration, trapezoidal
is the most straightforward,

00:51:48.780 --> 00:51:51.830
but it's most inefficient.

00:51:51.830 --> 00:51:58.040
So as I said that I'm
not a good at, really,

00:51:58.040 --> 00:52:00.650
numerics, the coding.

00:52:00.650 --> 00:52:04.020
But the one that I can reduce
the number of integration

00:52:04.020 --> 00:52:09.120
I know is if I use
this quadrature method.

00:52:09.120 --> 00:52:14.270
So if I want to use the
quadrature method fx dx,

00:52:14.270 --> 00:52:20.090
any integration, there is
one popular quadrature--

00:52:20.090 --> 00:52:21.380
not always good--

00:52:21.380 --> 00:52:25.220
I equals n, say, equals 1 to n.

00:52:25.220 --> 00:52:29.660
So this quadrature does not
divide the integration domain

00:52:29.660 --> 00:52:31.250
uniformly.

00:52:31.250 --> 00:52:34.190
You actually choose find
out the number of root.

00:52:34.190 --> 00:52:39.610
In this case, I have I equals 1
to n, so n any number you take.

00:52:39.610 --> 00:52:43.690
And it will find that the
optimal root of this quadrature

00:52:43.690 --> 00:52:45.260
and give away.

00:52:45.260 --> 00:52:47.770
So this is a xi.

00:52:47.770 --> 00:52:49.270
The i-- xi--

00:52:49.270 --> 00:52:53.830
this solution depends on
what quadrature you choose.

00:52:53.830 --> 00:52:58.190
And how many order your n is.

00:52:58.190 --> 00:53:03.150
And then you gave a
weight to that point.

00:53:03.150 --> 00:53:09.110
And so a popular way is the
Gauss-Legendre quadrature.

00:53:21.900 --> 00:53:24.690
So when I wrote
that, all my code

00:53:24.690 --> 00:53:27.990
was written using
Gauss-Legendre quadrature.

00:53:27.990 --> 00:53:29.640
And sometimes it works well.

00:53:29.640 --> 00:53:33.390
Other times, it may
not be that efficient.

00:53:33.390 --> 00:53:34.860
But there are other
quadrature you

00:53:34.860 --> 00:53:38.565
go to look at the numerical
integration books.

00:53:41.250 --> 00:53:47.010
And so with all this, the math
part, let's look at the result

00:53:47.010 --> 00:53:50.710
that you are supposed to
get, look at the solution,

00:53:50.710 --> 00:53:53.020
so what this solution look like.

00:53:53.020 --> 00:53:55.860
So I have the
problem I'm dealing

00:53:55.860 --> 00:53:59.910
with in the current [INAUDIBLE],,
the original configuration I

00:53:59.910 --> 00:54:01.500
draw, this is my y direction.

00:54:04.650 --> 00:54:07.565
And I use a
non-dimensional coordinate,

00:54:07.565 --> 00:54:13.950
eta equals y dy mean free path.

00:54:13.950 --> 00:54:21.650
And so if I plot the solution,
for this non-dimensionary

00:54:21.650 --> 00:54:28.200
intensity theta
eta, and I'm going

00:54:28.200 --> 00:54:32.160
to actually not use the eta.

00:54:32.160 --> 00:54:34.620
Here is doubly confusing.

00:54:34.620 --> 00:54:40.230
What I'm going to
do is y over d so

00:54:40.230 --> 00:54:45.900
that I can plot all these
lines between 0 to 1.

00:54:45.900 --> 00:54:48.630
d is the thickness of the plate.

00:54:48.630 --> 00:54:51.345
So I have 0 to 1.

00:54:55.320 --> 00:55:00.290
And this non-dimensional,
so the first is 1.

00:55:00.290 --> 00:55:02.240
The maximum is 1.

00:55:02.240 --> 00:55:03.440
The minimum of 0.

00:55:03.440 --> 00:55:10.250
So one solution, this
is a Fourier solution

00:55:10.250 --> 00:55:18.470
when the mean free path is much
smaller than the thickness.

00:55:18.470 --> 00:55:24.890
So what I have
here, this one is xi

00:55:24.890 --> 00:55:30.050
equals d over mean free path.

00:55:30.050 --> 00:55:34.130
So xi is the non-dimensional
thickness or mean free path

00:55:34.130 --> 00:55:38.020
when this one goes to infinite.

00:55:38.020 --> 00:55:41.080
That's a thickness much larger
than the mean free path.

00:55:41.080 --> 00:55:43.600
And this profile is linear.

00:55:43.600 --> 00:55:46.390
And that's what you would
expect from a Fourier

00:55:46.390 --> 00:55:47.830
law of heat conduction.

00:55:47.830 --> 00:55:54.680
This is diffusive, the local
diffusion [INAUDIBLE] solution.

00:55:54.680 --> 00:56:00.810
Now, in the other case,
when this xi goes to 0,

00:56:00.810 --> 00:56:05.440
what it means there's
no scattering inside.

00:56:05.440 --> 00:56:06.820
You will be ballistic.

00:56:06.820 --> 00:56:11.650
So you will have
a solution flat.

00:56:11.650 --> 00:56:18.610
It's going to 0.5.

00:56:18.610 --> 00:56:25.060
So this is the outer
limit when xi goes to 0.

00:56:25.060 --> 00:56:27.430
You have ballistic transport.

00:56:27.430 --> 00:56:29.560
So why is that?

00:56:29.560 --> 00:56:33.010
And although I say
here I'm solving

00:56:33.010 --> 00:56:36.370
using the phonon
as example, but I

00:56:36.370 --> 00:56:39.860
say this is valid for
electron and for molecule,

00:56:39.860 --> 00:56:42.320
for phonon and for photon.

00:56:42.320 --> 00:56:46.090
So let me take the
example I talked before.

00:56:46.090 --> 00:56:53.080
This is the case when I have
radiation transfer between two

00:56:53.080 --> 00:56:56.090
parallel plate in a vacuum.

00:56:56.090 --> 00:56:58.250
There's no scattering
inside for photon.

00:56:58.250 --> 00:57:02.120
Because xi is, say, goes to 0.

00:57:02.120 --> 00:57:06.320
That means d is very small
compared to mean free path.

00:57:06.320 --> 00:57:08.750
There's no internal scattering.

00:57:08.750 --> 00:57:10.490
When there is no
internal scattering,

00:57:10.490 --> 00:57:12.500
what you have here--

00:57:12.500 --> 00:57:16.340
recall, we said that here
is sigma T1 fourth power.

00:57:16.340 --> 00:57:20.900
Here is sigma T2 fourth power.

00:57:20.900 --> 00:57:26.000
That's what you will get
if you do I0 integration.

00:57:26.000 --> 00:57:32.450
Recall we did the
I0 if you put the--

00:57:32.450 --> 00:57:35.570
for black-body radiation
integration from 0 to infinite,

00:57:35.570 --> 00:57:37.190
you get sigma T fourth power.

00:57:37.190 --> 00:57:41.070
That's the Stefan-Boltzmann law.

00:57:41.070 --> 00:57:46.690
So in this case, I do not have
any scattering in between.

00:57:46.690 --> 00:57:49.410
I do not have
equilibrium between--

00:57:49.410 --> 00:57:51.650
very non-equilibrium.

00:57:51.650 --> 00:57:57.830
But I'm forcing my Boltzmann
solution to get a temperature.

00:57:57.830 --> 00:58:03.530
This temperature is the
equivalent temperature

00:58:03.530 --> 00:58:10.290
when I have this
imaginary absorber that

00:58:10.290 --> 00:58:13.890
absorb, on this side, absorbs
sigma T to fourth power,

00:58:13.890 --> 00:58:17.950
on the other side absorb
sigma T1 fourth power.

00:58:17.950 --> 00:58:21.190
So equivalently, the
absorber temperature

00:58:21.190 --> 00:58:24.790
will be the average of this
two fourth power divided by 2.

00:58:27.300 --> 00:58:29.320
And everywhere is the same.

00:58:29.320 --> 00:58:33.840
So that's why you say, in this
limit, my solution is flat.

00:58:37.250 --> 00:58:40.850
And now, in between,
I have a jump

00:58:40.850 --> 00:58:45.420
at this continuity
at both sides.

00:58:45.420 --> 00:58:50.070
And this will be almost
linear, not exactly.

00:58:50.070 --> 00:58:53.550
This is where it's
in between when you

00:58:53.550 --> 00:58:55.590
go between 0 and the infinite.

00:58:55.590 --> 00:59:00.660
That depends on how
large is the xi.

00:59:00.660 --> 00:59:05.760
So what's interesting out
of this is, at the boundary,

00:59:05.760 --> 00:59:08.025
there's a temperature
discontinuity,

00:59:08.025 --> 00:59:09.300
a temperature jump.

00:59:13.390 --> 00:59:17.770
So this is-- originally,
this is, say, T1 is here.

00:59:17.770 --> 00:59:19.330
That's normalized to 1.

00:59:19.330 --> 00:59:23.170
But see, what I say here is
that inside the material, when

00:59:23.170 --> 00:59:27.160
I use the Fourier law to find
the equivalent temperature when

00:59:27.160 --> 00:59:29.998
there is scattering,
you want at the wall,

00:59:29.998 --> 00:59:31.540
the temperature--
local temperature--

00:59:31.540 --> 00:59:33.100
is different from T1.

00:59:36.460 --> 00:59:39.220
So this temperature
discontinuity, temperature

00:59:39.220 --> 00:59:44.440
jump, is a very tricky point.

00:59:50.280 --> 00:59:53.640
And when we discuss chapter
5, I mentioned this.

00:59:53.640 --> 00:59:56.040
And here, it reappear.

00:59:56.040 --> 00:59:58.710
Because my solution
now is more general.

00:59:58.710 --> 01:00:00.240
I can solve for any.

01:00:00.240 --> 01:00:02.535
In the chapter 5, there's
no internal scatter,

01:00:02.535 --> 01:00:03.900
and I see there's a jump.

01:00:03.900 --> 01:00:07.620
That's 0.5 from 1 to 0.5.

01:00:07.620 --> 01:00:13.440
So what I'm dealing
here is first,

01:00:13.440 --> 01:00:16.560
it depends on what
problem you're solving.

01:00:16.560 --> 01:00:19.710
If it's a radiation
problem, this is physical.

01:00:19.710 --> 01:00:24.740
If a conduction problem, what
I'm talking is unphysical.

01:00:24.740 --> 01:00:27.700
It's the wrong definition.

01:00:27.700 --> 01:00:29.620
So why I say that?

01:00:29.620 --> 01:00:32.440
Now, let's go back to
think about the radiation.

01:00:32.440 --> 01:00:33.880
I mentioned this before--

01:00:33.880 --> 01:00:35.950
here, again.

01:00:35.950 --> 01:00:39.580
So I have this
imaginary detector.

01:00:39.580 --> 01:00:44.350
I can put there, on one side,
it'll absorb one type of photon

01:00:44.350 --> 01:00:47.410
as a T2 and the other side, T1.

01:00:47.410 --> 01:00:50.320
So once I go to
the wall, it still

01:00:50.320 --> 01:00:53.690
absorb in this side
and the other side.

01:00:53.690 --> 01:00:57.460
And so far, I have
assumed the wall is black.

01:00:57.460 --> 01:01:02.050
What this black means is, on
this side, a photon comes in.

01:01:02.050 --> 01:01:05.620
They just go no reflection.

01:01:05.620 --> 01:01:06.650
That's black.

01:01:06.650 --> 01:01:08.620
Black is no reflection.

01:01:08.620 --> 01:01:10.880
Everything that's
come in is absorbed.

01:01:10.880 --> 01:01:13.660
So it's also transmission.

01:01:13.660 --> 01:01:17.500
That means the
transmission is 1.

01:01:17.500 --> 01:01:22.930
When transmission is 1, so
of course, this is solid.

01:01:22.930 --> 01:01:26.050
And solid, I don't have
a way to get into it.

01:01:26.050 --> 01:01:26.890
I put a detector.

01:01:26.890 --> 01:01:30.490
But imagine you've got this
magical detector I have.

01:01:30.490 --> 01:01:33.550
And if it moves
into solid but it

01:01:33.550 --> 01:01:35.470
doesn't interact
with the phonon--

01:01:35.470 --> 01:01:36.970
it doesn't interact
with a solid,

01:01:36.970 --> 01:01:40.630
they only measure the
light, it's photon--

01:01:40.630 --> 01:01:42.445
what will be the
temperature you measure?

01:01:46.400 --> 01:01:48.060
AUDIENCE: Incoming [INAUDIBLE]?

01:01:48.060 --> 01:01:51.660
GANG CHEN: So incoming is--
so here is here on this side

01:01:51.660 --> 01:01:54.990
and on this side, both sides.

01:01:54.990 --> 01:01:57.420
So it will be continuous.

01:01:57.420 --> 01:02:00.050
In fact, there will
be no jump if you

01:02:00.050 --> 01:02:03.620
can do that magic detector.

01:02:03.620 --> 01:02:06.170
And of course, the
solid itself, this

01:02:06.170 --> 01:02:11.000
photon will have absorption
and reemission in the solid.

01:02:11.000 --> 01:02:15.440
So what will happen is that
this is what you measure.

01:02:15.440 --> 01:02:19.330
The photon temperature
you would measure.

01:02:19.330 --> 01:02:21.490
But there, you don't
have that detector.

01:02:21.490 --> 01:02:25.100
What you have is a thermocouple.

01:02:25.100 --> 01:02:26.900
And then when you
put the thermocouple

01:02:26.900 --> 01:02:31.370
into this solid, what you
measure is a solid temperature.

01:02:31.370 --> 01:02:35.700
That's a phonon
lattice vibration.

01:02:35.700 --> 01:02:37.500
So this is my T1.

01:02:37.500 --> 01:02:39.870
What I say is that this
is a phonon temperature.

01:02:39.870 --> 01:02:42.390
And equivalently,
when they emit,

01:02:42.390 --> 01:02:47.530
I say this is half of this
space they emit as black body.

01:02:47.530 --> 01:02:50.460
So that's where my physical
temperature jump coming

01:02:50.460 --> 01:02:54.630
from in the case of photon.

01:02:54.630 --> 01:03:01.290
Now, when I do phonon and
if the surface, again,

01:03:01.290 --> 01:03:02.840
is the black, as
what I'm seeing--

01:03:02.840 --> 01:03:04.910
that's the [? median ?]
at the temperature T1,

01:03:04.910 --> 01:03:06.410
equilibrium temperature.

01:03:06.410 --> 01:03:08.270
So transmissivity is 1.

01:03:10.970 --> 01:03:14.720
So if there's no reflection--
this is material 1, material 2,

01:03:14.720 --> 01:03:19.010
material 3, in solid, I
typically can do multilayer

01:03:19.010 --> 01:03:22.190
structure--

01:03:22.190 --> 01:03:27.470
in that case, there should
really be no temperature jump.

01:03:27.470 --> 01:03:28.130
It's similar.

01:03:28.130 --> 01:03:34.950
You can actually
measure the temperature.

01:03:34.950 --> 01:03:38.190
Suppose you have nice way
to measure the lattice

01:03:38.190 --> 01:03:39.450
temperature.

01:03:39.450 --> 01:03:42.330
So there should be no jump.

01:03:42.330 --> 01:03:44.280
And this jump is
only because I'm

01:03:44.280 --> 01:03:50.590
assuming the phonon coming
out is at this temperature.

01:03:50.590 --> 01:03:55.980
What happens is that this is the
interface I discussed before.

01:03:55.980 --> 01:04:00.240
If you look at what
comes to this side is T2,

01:04:00.240 --> 01:04:03.600
and T1 is going out like this.

01:04:03.600 --> 01:04:06.240
But the T2, it
goes out like this

01:04:06.240 --> 01:04:09.590
because the lower temperature.

01:04:09.590 --> 01:04:12.590
So if you define
temperature consistently,

01:04:12.590 --> 01:04:16.060
you should not have that jump.

01:04:16.060 --> 01:04:19.870
This is a highly
non-equilibrium situation.

01:04:19.870 --> 01:04:22.330
There's no good
temperature definition.

01:04:26.260 --> 01:04:30.320
However, the other solution--

01:04:30.320 --> 01:04:33.190
again, you can go back
to rederive everything--

01:04:33.190 --> 01:04:37.580
that is, when your
surface is not black,

01:04:37.580 --> 01:04:45.540
when your surface has finite
transmission/reflection,

01:04:45.540 --> 01:04:49.620
this is where you'll actually
have a physical temperature

01:04:49.620 --> 01:04:53.240
discontinuity, at
the two interface.

01:04:53.240 --> 01:04:55.940
And this wall, if you
do Boltzmann equation,

01:04:55.940 --> 01:04:59.750
you can consistently
including everything.

01:04:59.750 --> 01:05:03.530
And in chapter 5,
I discuss this.

01:05:03.530 --> 01:05:05.840
It's really you
have to be careful

01:05:05.840 --> 01:05:09.000
how you define
temperature when you

01:05:09.000 --> 01:05:13.230
go to this ballistic or
quasi-ballistic regime.

01:05:13.230 --> 01:05:16.415
Otherwise, you may be
comparing apple and orange.

01:05:21.460 --> 01:05:22.390
Any questions?

01:05:25.760 --> 01:05:28.010
It takes a lot of time.

01:05:28.010 --> 01:05:31.190
People still continue
making those mistakes

01:05:31.190 --> 01:05:33.320
so if you go to read papers.

01:05:33.320 --> 01:05:36.050
And then many of them
don't distinguish

01:05:36.050 --> 01:05:39.870
the subtle difference.

01:05:39.870 --> 01:05:40.500
OK.

01:05:40.500 --> 01:05:43.630
So that's a case.

01:05:43.630 --> 01:05:45.720
So if you think
about what we talked

01:05:45.720 --> 01:05:49.530
is a transport along
a film, transport

01:05:49.530 --> 01:05:53.100
perpendicular to a film.

01:05:53.100 --> 01:05:57.130
I'm going to next tell
you a story about mixture.

01:05:57.130 --> 01:06:00.820
Now, transport them both along
and perpendicular to the film.

01:06:00.820 --> 01:06:02.290
What would be the
case like that?

01:06:06.340 --> 01:06:07.810
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:06:07.810 --> 01:06:10.260
[LAUGHTER]

01:06:10.260 --> 01:06:12.840
GANG CHEN: This is a fluid flow.

01:06:12.840 --> 01:06:16.520
Think about push a
fluid through a pipe,

01:06:16.520 --> 01:06:21.490
air molecules, a small pipe.

01:06:21.490 --> 01:06:23.540
And this is the Poiseuille flow.

01:06:23.540 --> 01:06:30.030
So let's look at the
case, simplest case,

01:06:30.030 --> 01:06:36.600
rarefied Poiseuille flow.

01:06:48.470 --> 01:06:51.680
So this is the pipe flow that
you are all familiar with.

01:06:51.680 --> 01:06:55.520
But in the limit of Poiseuille
flow, I say two parallel plate.

01:06:55.520 --> 01:06:59.700
Between two parallel plates,
I'm flowing fluids through.

01:06:59.700 --> 01:07:03.690
And if one plate is moving,
the other plate is stationary.

01:07:03.690 --> 01:07:05.870
That's a Couette flow.

01:07:05.870 --> 01:07:07.940
If it's pressure-driven,
both plates

01:07:07.940 --> 01:07:10.850
are stationary, that's
Poiseuille flow.

01:07:10.850 --> 01:07:13.250
You learn that in
fluid mechanics.

01:07:13.250 --> 01:07:16.340
So I'm going to
deal with the case

01:07:16.340 --> 01:07:20.510
where I have stationary plates.

01:07:20.510 --> 01:07:22.865
I have a fluid flow inside.

01:07:25.760 --> 01:07:33.650
And in this case, we wrote down
the Boltzmann equation df dx.

01:07:33.650 --> 01:07:35.540
This is my x direction.

01:07:35.540 --> 01:07:44.810
And my y vy df dy equals
f minus fd over tau.

01:07:44.810 --> 01:07:46.970
That's my relaxation time.

01:07:46.970 --> 01:07:53.010
And it's not the f0 because I
have a non-zero fluid velocity,

01:07:53.010 --> 01:07:54.000
average velocity.

01:07:54.000 --> 01:07:58.190
So it is displaced Maxwell
distribution, we said before.

01:07:58.190 --> 01:08:01.790
So this displaced
Maxwell distribution

01:08:01.790 --> 01:08:17.420
is n m 2 pi kBT 3 over 2
exponential m v. That's

01:08:17.420 --> 01:08:21.319
the random velocity minus
u, which is a function.

01:08:21.319 --> 01:08:24.319
In the case of laminar flow--

01:08:24.319 --> 01:08:30.920
it's 2-- u is a function only--

01:08:30.920 --> 01:08:36.260
u is changing in the x direction
but is a function of y.

01:08:36.260 --> 01:08:41.520
That's the pi flow
in normal parabola.

01:08:41.520 --> 01:08:44.670
And y is my coordinate.

01:08:44.670 --> 01:08:48.180
U is the flow, average
flow direction.

01:08:48.180 --> 01:08:55.640
So u is a function of y
but is in the x direction.

01:08:55.640 --> 01:08:58.800
So flow is in the x direction.

01:08:58.800 --> 01:09:03.210
My constraint is, again,
in the y direction.

01:09:03.210 --> 01:09:04.770
That's a finite thickness.

01:09:07.899 --> 01:09:10.090
And it's pressure-driven.

01:09:10.090 --> 01:09:12.310
So where does the
pressure come in?

01:09:12.310 --> 01:09:15.010
It doesn't come into
my Boltzmann equation.

01:09:15.010 --> 01:09:18.939
Pressure, in fact, is
a statistical average,

01:09:18.939 --> 01:09:21.640
just like temperature is
mechanical-- statistical

01:09:21.640 --> 01:09:23.979
average on the momentum.

01:09:23.979 --> 01:09:30.910
But in this case here, it can
relate to n is a function of x.

01:09:30.910 --> 01:09:38.050
And then you can use the
ideal gas as PV equals nRT.

01:09:38.050 --> 01:09:42.700
So this is-- you relate
this molecular density

01:09:42.700 --> 01:09:45.050
to the pressure.

01:09:45.050 --> 01:09:46.700
I have to say that
I'm not satisfied

01:09:46.700 --> 01:09:49.410
with this approximation.

01:09:49.410 --> 01:09:51.319
So ideally, I would
not even do that.

01:09:51.319 --> 01:09:54.740
I would say, OK, my pressure
is just a momentum average.

01:09:54.740 --> 01:09:59.720
I tried to do that, and
I was not successful.

01:09:59.720 --> 01:10:01.670
So this is the classical.

01:10:01.670 --> 01:10:04.370
You'll put in the
Boltzmann equation now.

01:10:04.370 --> 01:10:12.590
And both in the x
direction have u in x.

01:10:12.590 --> 01:10:16.580
I have the source term in
the x direction-- pressure.

01:10:16.580 --> 01:10:20.700
And in the y direction,
I have the boundary.

01:10:20.700 --> 01:10:23.960
And if you plug in
into the equation,

01:10:23.960 --> 01:10:25.640
you will find out
that you need to do

01:10:25.640 --> 01:10:30.530
the same thing as we did for
the perpendicular transport.

01:10:30.530 --> 01:10:35.030
Because u, that's a
velocity distribution

01:10:35.030 --> 01:10:37.850
is we do not know-- average
velocity distribution,

01:10:37.850 --> 01:10:40.850
just like when you try to find
temperature distribution. u is

01:10:40.850 --> 01:10:43.170
a function of y.

01:10:43.170 --> 01:10:46.740
And so u is inside the integral.

01:10:46.740 --> 01:10:49.170
It's an integral
equation over u.

01:10:49.170 --> 01:10:52.920
When you do
everything derivable,

01:10:52.920 --> 01:10:54.330
just follow the same way.

01:10:57.210 --> 01:11:02.520
So what is interesting
is out of this problem

01:11:02.520 --> 01:11:05.460
is a Knudsen minimum.

01:11:10.380 --> 01:11:14.540
So this was observed
experimentally

01:11:14.540 --> 01:11:21.030
in 1905 or 1906, this
period, by Knudsen

01:11:21.030 --> 01:11:24.210
doing rarefied gas flow.

01:11:24.210 --> 01:11:32.340
And what they observed
is if you plot your flux,

01:11:32.340 --> 01:11:36.630
say, mass flux,
which is normalized,

01:11:36.630 --> 01:11:42.990
say, 2R ideal gas constant
0 and the d square--

01:11:42.990 --> 01:11:45.600
d is the thickness
of the plate--

01:11:45.600 --> 01:11:50.280
and the real measure
the flux and dp dx.

01:11:50.280 --> 01:11:53.760
And so this is a
non-dimensional.

01:11:53.760 --> 01:11:57.720
And what it has is--

01:11:57.720 --> 01:12:00.720
and this is drawn as a
function of Knudsen number,

01:12:00.720 --> 01:12:02.340
which is a [INAUDIBLE].

01:12:02.340 --> 01:12:05.700
Knudsen number is a
mean free path over d.

01:12:05.700 --> 01:12:10.982
That xi, I mentioned before,
is d over mean free path.

01:12:10.982 --> 01:12:12.940
In different fields,
there are different names.

01:12:12.940 --> 01:12:17.640
For example, inverse
of this is, in optics,

01:12:17.640 --> 01:12:19.315
called optical thickness.

01:12:19.315 --> 01:12:21.780
In acoustic, we also
call acoustic thickness.

01:12:21.780 --> 01:12:26.280
But this inverse, mean free
path over d is a Knudsen number.

01:12:26.280 --> 01:12:28.870
And that's why it has
a longest history.

01:12:28.870 --> 01:12:31.510
So if you say Knudsen number
for phonon, Knudsen number

01:12:31.510 --> 01:12:33.340
for electron, that's all fine.

01:12:33.340 --> 01:12:36.240
You just need to follow
the same mean free path

01:12:36.240 --> 01:12:40.770
over the thickness, over
the [INAUDIBLE] thickness.

01:12:40.770 --> 01:12:43.155
There are people actually
call the wrong inverse

01:12:43.155 --> 01:12:44.250
as the Knudsen number.

01:12:44.250 --> 01:12:47.090
That's wrong.

01:12:47.090 --> 01:12:54.300
So what they find experimentally
is that it goes this way.

01:12:54.300 --> 01:12:55.340
And this is the 1.

01:12:55.340 --> 01:12:56.810
This is about 1.

01:12:56.810 --> 01:12:58.280
This is what happens
around the 1.

01:13:01.600 --> 01:13:05.330
And when you go to--

01:13:05.330 --> 01:13:08.170
so here, very small
Knudsen number.

01:13:08.170 --> 01:13:09.940
That's a continuum.

01:13:09.940 --> 01:13:14.840
Very large Knudsen number,
that's a rarefied gas flow.

01:13:14.840 --> 01:13:17.030
And in between,
there is a minimum.

01:13:17.030 --> 01:13:20.410
And this minimum called
the Knudsen minimum.

01:13:20.410 --> 01:13:24.230
This was experimentally
found, though.

01:13:24.230 --> 01:13:31.100
So I say, around 1900,
that there was no solution.

01:13:31.100 --> 01:13:35.360
People try to do approximate
solution Boltzmann equation.

01:13:35.360 --> 01:13:39.410
And it wasn't really
until 1960 or 1950,

01:13:39.410 --> 01:13:42.680
and there is a
great mathematician,

01:13:42.680 --> 01:13:44.270
and he solved the problem.

01:13:44.270 --> 01:13:48.920
Cercignani, he solved this
problem, Boltzmann equation

01:13:48.920 --> 01:13:52.700
this way I just written
down, like y direction,

01:13:52.700 --> 01:13:55.310
and you'll find the velocity.

01:13:55.310 --> 01:13:57.530
And they found that,
indeed, this solution

01:13:57.530 --> 01:14:00.140
has a Knudsen minimum.

01:14:00.140 --> 01:14:03.890
And the reason for
this, I tried to come up

01:14:03.890 --> 01:14:07.970
with a very direct picture.

01:14:07.970 --> 01:14:09.560
And the reason is this.

01:14:09.560 --> 01:14:16.240
You have a pressure-driven
flux going in the x direction.

01:14:16.240 --> 01:14:18.300
And then we say in
heat conduction,

01:14:18.300 --> 01:14:23.940
the roughness always decrease
the thermal conductivity.

01:14:23.940 --> 01:14:26.550
But see, here, actually,
you got a regime

01:14:26.550 --> 01:14:31.920
where in the very dilute regime,
your pressure is pressing

01:14:31.920 --> 01:14:35.640
these fluids much faster.

01:14:35.640 --> 01:14:38.990
So the boundary
effect is actually,

01:14:38.990 --> 01:14:42.140
the scattering at the
boundary, is less important.

01:14:42.140 --> 01:14:49.010
And that's why you have
the recovery of this, say,

01:14:49.010 --> 01:14:51.770
flux, the fluid mass flux there.

01:14:55.180 --> 01:14:57.820
And then, corresponding
to this solution,

01:14:57.820 --> 01:15:03.570
you go to look at your velocity
distribution in the pipe

01:15:03.570 --> 01:15:05.850
or in the parallel plate.

01:15:05.850 --> 01:15:09.990
You'll actually find-- this
is where the name actually

01:15:09.990 --> 01:15:12.240
come from.

01:15:12.240 --> 01:15:17.250
We know that, typically,
when we do fluid problem,

01:15:17.250 --> 01:15:21.530
you see velocity is a
non-slip boundary condition.

01:15:21.530 --> 01:15:24.260
But the Boltzmann equation
will tell you-- actually,

01:15:24.260 --> 01:15:25.490
this is 0--

01:15:25.490 --> 01:15:29.600
the velocity has a
discontinuity at the wall.

01:15:29.600 --> 01:15:31.010
So this is the velocity.

01:15:31.010 --> 01:15:36.640
There is actually slip happen
between the fluid and the wall.

01:15:39.310 --> 01:15:43.540
So this is the
slip, velocity slip.

01:15:43.540 --> 01:15:45.400
And in the next
lecture, we'll actually

01:15:45.400 --> 01:15:47.800
discuss people have
tried to [INAUDIBLE]..

01:15:47.800 --> 01:15:50.020
Obviously, if you do
rarefy flow or you

01:15:50.020 --> 01:15:52.840
do fluid mechanics
and micro fluids,

01:15:52.840 --> 01:15:57.370
you'll add artificial
velocity slip.

01:15:57.370 --> 01:16:00.520
And in fact, you add the
first-order and second-order

01:16:00.520 --> 01:16:05.240
velocity slip to
try to accommodate.

01:16:05.240 --> 01:16:07.090
It turns out that the
first-order velocity

01:16:07.090 --> 01:16:10.030
slip typically can
go to this regime.

01:16:10.030 --> 01:16:14.230
It cannot go, say, Knudsen
equals 1 or you want higher.

01:16:14.230 --> 01:16:18.450
Second-order velocity
slip, you will overpredict

01:16:18.450 --> 01:16:21.430
the velocities, they Knudsen--

01:16:21.430 --> 01:16:23.740
this range.

01:16:23.740 --> 01:16:27.370
So even those states, there
are people still trying

01:16:27.370 --> 01:16:29.350
to write the boundary condition.

01:16:29.350 --> 01:16:32.380
And I'll tell you a story how I
derive a new boundary condition

01:16:32.380 --> 01:16:33.520
next lecture.

01:16:33.520 --> 01:16:41.740
But this is the Knudsen flow
and the rarefied Poiseuille flow

01:16:41.740 --> 01:16:44.230
Knudsen minimum.

01:16:44.230 --> 01:16:46.580
And let me tell you a story.

01:16:46.580 --> 01:16:51.340
I mentioned many times
that the disk drive,

01:16:51.340 --> 01:16:53.650
the disk drive industry.

01:16:53.650 --> 01:16:57.350
And the disk drive is
a lubrication problem.

01:16:57.350 --> 01:17:02.110
So what it has is
this is a disk.

01:17:02.110 --> 01:17:06.740
Lubrication problem is
typical [INAUDIBLE] equation.

01:17:06.740 --> 01:17:08.900
And the [INAUDIBLE]
question, how it is

01:17:08.900 --> 01:17:10.610
is basically the rotating disk.

01:17:10.610 --> 01:17:12.590
Here's the magnetic head.

01:17:12.590 --> 01:17:16.430
And this magnetic head
has an inclination.

01:17:16.430 --> 01:17:18.790
So it's like an airplane.

01:17:18.790 --> 01:17:21.270
You have angle, so
that gives you lift.

01:17:21.270 --> 01:17:22.680
And the lift is very important.

01:17:22.680 --> 01:17:28.080
Because otherwise, your
header will crash the disk.

01:17:28.080 --> 01:17:31.470
And this disk will
rotate about 10,000 RPM--

01:17:31.470 --> 01:17:33.450
very fast.

01:17:33.450 --> 01:17:35.160
So about 10 years
ago, I have a friend.

01:17:35.160 --> 01:17:37.620
They said, can you
look at this problem?

01:17:37.620 --> 01:17:44.220
We have 1 nanometer separation
that we cannot predict.

01:17:44.220 --> 01:17:46.080
And that 1 nanometer,
they already--

01:17:46.080 --> 01:17:49.960
[INAUDIBLE] they have
not used the continuum.

01:17:49.960 --> 01:17:53.170
They actually use the Boltzmann
equation solution for the flux.

01:17:53.170 --> 01:17:56.880
So they used
Cercignani's solution.

01:17:56.880 --> 01:18:00.930
Because this problem is,
so you're stationary.

01:18:00.930 --> 01:18:04.230
So you can roughly say
this is your moment plane.

01:18:04.230 --> 01:18:05.970
It's a combination of the two.

01:18:05.970 --> 01:18:06.720
It's pressure.

01:18:06.720 --> 01:18:09.330
Because when you have an angle--
if you don't have an angle,

01:18:09.330 --> 01:18:10.635
that's a Couette flow.

01:18:10.635 --> 01:18:14.580
When you have an angle, you have
a pressure difference build up.

01:18:14.580 --> 01:18:16.560
So the pressure were
Poiseuille flow.

01:18:16.560 --> 01:18:19.290
So it's a superposition
of Couette

01:18:19.290 --> 01:18:21.950
and the Poiseuille flow.

01:18:21.950 --> 01:18:27.170
So they have been using
the Cercignani solution.

01:18:27.170 --> 01:18:29.720
And there was a famous paper
for this disk drive problem

01:18:29.720 --> 01:18:32.000
that decomposing.

01:18:32.000 --> 01:18:34.820
But they say there is
a 1-nanometer problem.

01:18:34.820 --> 01:18:35.900
I said, what cares?

01:18:35.900 --> 01:18:37.650
1 nanometer-- why you need it?

01:18:37.650 --> 01:18:40.640
It turns out that because,
at the time I mentioned,

01:18:40.640 --> 01:18:44.900
they were about 20
nanometer flying height.

01:18:44.900 --> 01:18:47.390
And now this flying height
I mentioned also to you

01:18:47.390 --> 01:18:50.460
is about 1.5 nanometer.

01:18:50.460 --> 01:18:55.230
OK, that's why it's such
an important problem.

01:18:55.230 --> 01:18:58.050
We'll never find out,
though, what's the reason.

01:18:58.050 --> 01:18:59.370
It could be the molecular.

01:18:59.370 --> 01:19:01.650
It could be absorb the molecule.

01:19:01.650 --> 01:19:04.590
It could be the molecular
[INAUDIBLE] size of molecules.

01:19:07.400 --> 01:19:12.200
But it's a very relevant
technology problem.

01:19:12.200 --> 01:19:14.150
And nowadays, like
I say, there's

01:19:14.150 --> 01:19:17.470
still concern because
you still have disk,

01:19:17.470 --> 01:19:21.360
and you have a slider there.

01:19:21.360 --> 01:19:24.740
And finally, there
is another problem.

01:19:24.740 --> 01:19:26.620
You can think about this.

01:19:26.620 --> 01:19:31.510
If I have T hot and T cold,
I have a small opening.

01:19:31.510 --> 01:19:35.860
So this opening is smaller
than molecular mean free path.

01:19:35.860 --> 01:19:38.230
The question is, what's
the flux direction?

01:19:40.750 --> 01:19:45.340
What's the mass flux going,
from cold to hot or hot to cold?

01:19:50.360 --> 01:19:52.640
Maybe we'll discuss
in the next lecture.

01:19:52.640 --> 01:19:54.410
You can think about this.

01:19:54.410 --> 01:19:56.180
I'll ask you at the beginning.

01:19:56.180 --> 01:19:57.140
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:19:57.140 --> 01:19:57.765
GANG CHEN: Huh?

01:19:57.765 --> 01:19:59.540
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:19:59.540 --> 01:20:02.350
GANG CHEN: I want
the [INAUDIBLE] flux.

